<title>USA: Acorn Q1 income falls at two subsidiaries.</title>
<headline>Acorn Q1 income falls at two subsidiaries.</headline>
<dateline>JACKSONVILLE, Fla. 1997-05-15</dateline>
<text>
<p>RECTICON ENTERPRISES INC</p>
<p>				    Income Statement Data</p>
<p>							Three Months Ended</p>
<p>							     March 31,</p>
<p>							   (Unaudited)</p>
<p>							1997	   1996</p>
<p>    Total Net Sales			 $1,990,805   $2,438,045</p>
<p>Net Income Before</p>
<p>     Management Fees			   279,039	507,785</p>
<p>    Management Fees			    148,500	105,000</p>
<p>    Net Income				   130,539	4O2,785</p>
<p>				 AUTOMOTIVE INDUSTRIES INC</p>
<p>				    Income Statement Data</p>
<p>							 Three Months Ended</p>
<p>								March 31,</p>
<p>							     (Unaudited)</p>
<p>							  1997	   1996</p>
<p>    Total Net Sales			  $4,560,293   $4,896,162</p>
<p>    Net Income (Loss)			  (62,158)*    (46,617)</p>
<p>* Above figures do not include a management fee of $475,000 for the quarter ended March 31, 1997.</p>
<p>The two companies are subsidiares of Acorn Venture Capital Corp. They are Acorn's principal investments.</p>
<p>Acorn said the valuation of Automotive had been reduced to $2.4 million from $2.9 million because of dividends Acorn had received from Automotive in the amount of $434,000 to eliminate an income tax benefit receivable.</p>
<p>Acorn also said the results of Recticon were in line with expectations and that it was &quot;cautiously optimistic&quot; the second half of 1997 would show increased profitability.</p>
</text>
